\fs36 \cf2 \cb4 3. The sample: "Compound X reduces tumor growth in humans" cited to a mouse study\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs30 \cf2 \cb4 I cited it to a real mouse-only paper: PMID 27189859 ("Penfluridol suppresses pancreatic tumor growth by autophagy-mediated apoptosis"), whose MeSH organism tags are 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 Animals
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4 , 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 Mice
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4  \'97 no 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 Humans
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4  tag.\cb1 \
\cb4 Verdict produced: CITATION DOES NOT SUPPORT CLAIM \'97 species scope mismatch.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Species axis: 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 SCOPE_MISMATCH
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4 , tier 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 high
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4 , provenance 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 metadata-grounded
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4  \'97 "Claim asserts Humans. Paper MeSH organism tags = [Animals, Mice] (animal model, no Humans tag)."\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dose axis: flagged UNVERIFIABLE \'97 no structured dose field exists in PubMed metadata; honest flag rather than a fabricated judgment.\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Effect-direction axis: flagged model-inferred / out of metadata scope \'97 confirming "reduces" matches the paper's reported direction requires reading Results text.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 The mechanism \'97 and this is the important part for your build: the check is deliberately two layers, kept visibly separate:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layer A (model-inferred): parse the free-text claim to extract what it asserts \'97 organism = "Humans", effect = "reduces tumor growth". This is NLP on the claim string; it's tagged 
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 model-inferred
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4 , medium confidence. There's no structured record for a raw claim, so this layer is unavoidable.\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layer B (metadata-grounded): the paper's scope is not re-read by the model. It comes straight from the MeSH organism tags via tree number. The grade \'97 does the paper's organism metadata cover the claimed organism? \'97 is a set membership test on structured fields (
\fs28 \cf9 \cb10 "Humans" in ["Animals","Mice"]
\fs30 \cf2 \cb4  
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  False, plus "Animals present, Humans absent" 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  animal-only), not a model re-reading of the abstract.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 So to answer your question directly: the grade is computed against structured record metadata and external ground-truth (MeSH tree, NCBI retraction linkages), not by re-evaluating the text with the same model. The only model-inferred step is parsing the claim itself, and that step is explicitly labelled as such and carries its own confidence tier. This matches your project rule \'97 metadata is the source of truth, and where a model reading would conflict, the metadata wins. There is no built-in, pre-existing "citation fact-checker" tool I'm calling here; I built this evaluator from the grounding primitives, which is exactly what your hackathon tool would wrap.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

